nt  everything that exists that's alive  has a history embedded  in its many adaptations that it had to  make to get here  um you know if we look at a plant now  it's almost  unrecognizable to the very first land  plants that  we see in the church of scotland the  rhine  shirt um and so it's often hard to say  like how are these things related  and those were a product of their  environment those soils were different  everything was very different then  and so um so we can use those analogies  to really guide that question um  when we look at other worlds we can say  again like  what sort of analogous metabolisms what  energy sources  are available there um what solvent  sources are available there or even  um more specific things about  temperature and  and radiation which isn't always a bad  thing right that that could be  this power that we get from the sun as  well and so just use those kinds  of of really basic parameters of an  environment  to then say okay what is the possibility  space that life could have  in that kind of condition as well  all right so if we think then about our  closest planetary neighbor which is  mars we know that for example earth  hosts an abundance of life but we still  don't know if there is  or ever was life on mars so  how did these two planets diverge in  their evolution and what does that teach  us  about life so i love  thinking about mars not necessarily  mars as it is now when i think about  bio signatures and possibility for life  and if there's life  elsewhere because mars is a really  special case  um if we look in the past when  um life was first originating here on  this planet  mars wasn't terribly different from  earth  it had an ocean it's not that far from  the sun it was getting a good amount of  sunlight  um it had a protective atmosphere so it  didn't have the  radiation problems at the surface that  it does now that are really harmful  and it was it was colder but it's  basically like  earth's little colder siste