er and bigger  and bigger  in this abiotic system of reactions that  are happening  really great example on this planet  would be chlorophyll  that's a fantastically complicated  molecule that's really hard to make if  you're not a plant  um but we're swimming in it and we have  way more of it than anything else that's  that's not the kind of uh chemistry that  physics would make  you can extend that same analogy to all  sorts of systems  you know where if you're even thinking  about elemental abundance  nature puts elements in different um  abundance patterns than life does and so  really that's what we're doing in  agnostic biosignatures  and that goes back to that other thing i  was saying is really  understanding your abiotic environment  is so  important so you know when you're  looking at something that's unlikely  and also you have a sense of what the  the energy barrier between this  strange thing you're seeing and the rest  of the environment are and that will  give you a clue  if it's truly something that's living  interesting and so i guess one of the  questions i have next  is does the search for life depend  on what our definition of life is and  what we consider to be life  yeah this this is a tricky question  because of course nobody can agree what  life  is right and um  and i i think one way that i like to say  that is like  physicists also don't describe what they  do they're like oh my gosh there's this  crazy new  you know particle we don't we don't know  what it is and nobody's like no  define energy what is that particle  so i feel like in life sciences and in  life detection  um it's okay that we don't have a  definition  of of what is the canonical part of life  and that's why i'm i'm just fine  saying life is everything that isn't  abiotic until you prove it otherwise  um because i think also we get really  trapped in this kind of taxonomy of  what's life and we miss out on a lot of  the really creative questions  and we really um damage the