n the oceans  that  are raining down weathered rock material  from the continents  from life that's in the oceans and  slowly building up this history  and eventually those oceans move on  we've we've seen  you know i'm sure everyone has seen old  globes of what the world looked like  when there was gondwana and other  you know super continents so it's no  surprise the oceans  aren't always in the same location over  the 4 billion year old history of the  earth  so what happens is those oceans see that  seabed becomes the record of what used  to live  there that happens in lakes that happens  in all sorts of different geologic  systems  and so that that package of geology  becomes that  time machine for going back and looking  and asking what  lived there you can do that just by  looking at the rock it can tell you  things like  was that water oxygenated or not because  certainly our oceans haven't always had  oxygen and our atmosphere didn't either  so we can tell really really um broad  questions like that when we look at  those rocks  or we can look at really specific things  we can look for specific fossils  um did that you know when did shells  arrive  shell on organisms was a massive  innovation and evolution of protection  for organisms when did those arise  or we can look at really super specific  things like a remnant of that  chlorophyll i talked about we find that  all over in the rocks  you know photosynthesis has been a you  know  a big industry on this planet and we can  find that in the rocks as well so that's  kind of what i mean when i say looking  back in time  interesting and so then to to take  another question from our audience which  really connects this to  to other worlds um  when you look at the earth what are you  learning through the earth 4d  programming through your studies here on  earth  about um about what the connections are  between earth and mars so like what are  you looking for  when you look at the earth that helps  you connect 