lete web  on our planet um and so looking at a  place where there isn't life  answers as many questions um as  as looking for life on earth it really  tells us like oh what were the limiting  things  um between the two planets so i know it  sounds a little bit disappointing but  i'm  almost always even more excited to know  to see that mars doesn't have life i  also  should say that from a geologist's point  of view it's even more exciting to see a  planet  with light without life so that you can  understand a lot of the processes that  would be completely  physical there are all sorts of  processes on earth that have life as an  intermediary and so it makes it really  hard to understand  how would those processes look in a  completely abiotic sense because we  can't do those experiments here on earth  you try  and something moves in and next thing  you know you have a bunch of critters  um so we can look at weathering  questions  and and questions about mineral states  on mars in a very different way than we  can do those experiments  in environments here on earth where  inevitably something's going to move in  and add a whole new signal to your  system  indeed we have a lot of life here on  earth  so we've talked a lot i guess about the  similarities and the differences between  earth and mars but there is an enormous  diversity of worlds in our solar system  so what do those look like and would we  expect to find life on those other  worlds in our solar system  yeah so i think a really useful way to  approach this question is to first make  sure everyone knows  that as much as we like to talk about  water on mars  there is a lot of water in our solar  system  there are many moons in the outer solar  system which have extant oceans oceans  on them right now  and these are very different  oceans than our own they're often  encrusted in ice  and as far as we can tell  our ocean is the least salty ocean  in the solar system so this is where it  becomes really useful 