er and bigger  and bigger  in this abiotic system of reactions that  are happening  really great example on this planet  would be chlorophyll  that's a fantastically complicated  molecule that's really hard to make if  you're not a plant  um but we're swimming in it and we have  way more of it than anything else that's  that's not the kind of uh chemistry that  physics would make  you can extend that same analogy to all  sorts of systems  you know where if you're even thinking  about elemental abundance  nature puts elements in different um  abundance patterns than life does and so  really that's what we're doing in  agnostic biosignatures  and that goes back to that other thing i  was saying is really  understanding your abiotic environment  is so  important so you know when you're  looking at something that's unlikely  and also you have a sense of what the  the energy barrier between this  strange thing you're seeing and the rest  of the environment are and that will  give you a clue  if it's truly something that's living  interesting and so i guess one of the  questions i have next  is does the search for life depend  on what our definition of life is and  what we consider to be life  yeah this this is a tricky question  because of course nobody can agree what  life  is right and um  and i i think one way that i like to say  that is like  physicists also don't describe what they  do they're like oh my gosh there's this  crazy new  you know particle we don't we don't know  what it is and nobody's like no  define energy what is that particle  so i feel like in life sciences and in  life detection  um it's okay that we don't have a  definition  of of what is the canonical part of life  and that's why i'm i'm just fine  saying life is everything that isn't  abiotic until you prove it otherwise  um because i think also we get really  trapped in this kind of taxonomy of  what's life and we miss out on a lot of  the really creative questions  and we really um damage the  possibilities of our own research  i think a really prescient example  that's probably on a lot of people's  minds right now and why we're talking  like this and on a coffee shop  is that you know by a lot of definitions  of virus  isn't alive and i'm not gonna wade into  that  fight because that's not my area um but  i think  by treating something like that as not  alive  you've already blocked out certain tools  that could have been adapted  for that study that would have been very  advantageous had there just been a  simple  taxonomic shift in how we think about it  and this is something that's very  important for us in in agnostic  biosignatures  is to never leave any tool behind um  just because it's something that we  don't use for detecting life here on  earth doesn't mean that it might not be  a good tool for using in the distant  past when we're trying to  understand life um it preserved  uh elements of life here on this planet  or in a completely different system  potential ecosystem like another planet  fantastic and so thank you heather for  sharing your insights and for answering  some of these first few questions  and i think perhaps now i'll turn to  some of the questions from the audience  so and this is a good segue from what  you just said but you've talked a bit  about how we  look back in time using earth or other  places as examples  so can you say a little bit more about  what you mean in terms of looking back  in time and  how it's possible to do that yeah  so that's a great question and i know it  sounds a little bit flipped to say  looking back in time and i think the  best example that i can give you is  geology is our window into the distant  past  um the the writer  ecologist rachel carson had a great book  that she wrote which nobody remembers  because silent spring was so great  but she wrote a book called the oceans  around us and one of the things that she  talks about  is the fact that the sediments um  are this constant snowfall i