ng environments radically  different than on earth for example  purely non-organic chemistries or  nuclear reaction driven  driven life so do you think we have ways  right now to research  that possibility or is it clearly  impossible  yeah that's a that's a good question you  know and that's a that's a real question  right now  um because in i don't know now i can't  remember in six  short years um we're going to be sending  a spacecraft to go to titan  and titan doesn't have a water ocean  that's a hydrocarbon ocean think of it  as  like um rubbing alcohol or gasoline or  something like that  you know that's not a water ocean and  everything we see here on life  all this life on earth we see living in  association with water somehow  um and so that means that when we go to  titan we're really going to have to  either be thinking really hard about  refugia for life  that we're familiar with that needs  water or radically different chemistries  which would basically flip everything on  on its side  we live in in a water solvent enriched  environment water is in all of our cells  and then we take hydrocarbons  and build around the water um  to make our bodies microbes do the same  thing you'd have to flip that chemistry  on its side  on titan so that's that's a you have to  get really creative  in how you think about biochemistry and  this is where i think it actually  becomes really useful to start again and  i know this sounds boring looking at the  places where life doesn't live  um to to try and understand a little bit  more about what becomes  the the limiting of uh  factors for life to exist somewhere  because the real problem with not having  water  is something that does exist here on  earth not just in like oil spills  but um in brines where water is  much less available to an organism just  because a brine has lots of water  doesn't mean it's available  because all of those water molecules are  really being  taken up by all of the salts that are in  that wa